'''Montserrat Riera i Sola''' (born 3 March 1948) is a Balboan film director, screenwriter, and editor, widely regarded as the leading feminist filmmaker of the [[Gilded age of Balboan cinema]]. Born in [[Sant Bart]] and based in [[Sant Cristòfor]] since the early 1970s, Riera i Sola began her career as a scriptwriter for [[Cinema Lliure]] before leaving the mainstream studio to make independent films that addressed subjects the mainstream would not finance. Her second film, ''[[El Desig (film)|El Desig]]'' (1987), a feminist depiction of the sexual and economic lives of three women during the [[Balboa real-estate bubble|real-estate bubble]], was the first frank portrayal of female sexuality in a Balboan film and triggered the landmark constitutional case [[Balboa v. Riera i Sola]], in which the [[Constitutional Court of Balboa]] struck down the [[Public Decency Act]] as incompatible with the [[Twelve Guarantees]]. Her subsequent film, ''[[La Llibertat de les Dones (film)|La Llibertat de les Dones]]'' (1991), is regarded as the most accomplished feminist film of the era.

== Early life ==

Montserrat Riera Sola was born on 3 March 1948 in [[Sant Bart]], the capital of [[Jala Province]], in the industrial highlands of interior Balboa. Her father, Bernat Riera i Mas, was a textile worker at the Yalabirga mills, and her mother, Caterina Solà i Verdaguer, was a primary schoolteacher. Riera i Sola was the eldest of three children. She grew up in a working-class household shaped by the industrial economy of the Jala highlands, where the textile mills and the associated labour movement were the dominant social forces.

Riera i Sola attended the Escola Pública de Sant Bart, where her mother taught, and subsequently the Institut de Sant Bart, the province's principal secondary school. She was an academically able student with an interest in literature and writing. The cultural life of Sant Bart in the 1960s was limited — the city had no cinema and the theatre scene was small — but Riera i Sola read widely and was exposed to Volisanian and Mesogean literature through the school library and the local cultural society.

The conservative social environment of Sant Bart shaped Riera i Sola's early consciousness of gender and class. The interior provinces, under the influence of the [[Balboan Catholic Church]] and the planter oligarchy's patriarchal traditions, maintained strict social norms regarding women's roles, and the industrial working class of Jala, though more secular than the rural interior, was not immune to these expectations. Riera i Sola's decision to leave Sant Bart for Sant Cristòfor in 1970, at the age of twenty-two, was driven in part by a desire to escape the constraints of the interior's social order and to pursue the cultural and economic opportunities of the capital.

== Cinema Lliure years ==

Riera i Sola moved to [[Sant Cristòfor]] in 1970 and found work in the harbour-front district, where the post-reunification cultural revival was producing new opportunities in the creative industries. She worked briefly as a clerk at a shipping company before securing a position as a script assistant at [[Cinema Lliure]] in 1972, one of the principal studios of the [[Silver age of Balboan cinema|Silver age]]. The Silver age's low-budget, high-output production model provided opportunities for writers and technicians to learn the craft of filmmaking through practical experience, and Riera i Sola advanced from script assistant to scriptwriter within three years.

At Cinema Lliure, Riera i Sola wrote scripts for several of the studio's social comedies and documentaries between 1972 and 1977. The work provided her with technical training in narrative structure, dialogue, and the practical logistics of film production, but she became increasingly frustrated with the studio's refusal to finance films that addressed women's lives with the directness she considered necessary. The mainstream studios of the Silver age and the emerging Gilded age addressed social issues — race relations, political corruption, labour conditions — but treated women's sexuality and economic independence as peripheral subjects, subordinate to the male-centred narratives that the studios considered commercially viable. Riera i Sola proposed several scripts that addressed women's reproductive rights, domestic labour, and economic precarity, all of which were rejected by the studio's production committee on the grounds that they lacked commercial potential.

The growing [[Balboa real-estate bubble|real-estate bubble]] of the late 1970s intensified Riera i Sola's disillusionment with the mainstream industry. The surge of capital into the film industry produced larger budgets and a shift toward commercially oriented, star-driven productions that further marginalised the kind of socially engaged, low-budget filmmaking that Riera i Sola wanted to pursue. In 1977, she left Cinema Lliure to make films independently.

== Independent career ==

=== ''Les Dones del Port'' (1981) ===

Riera i Sola's first film as an independent filmmaker was ''[[Les Dones del Port (film)|Les Dones del Port]]'' ("The Women of the Port", 1981), a documentary about the women who worked in the harbour district's informal economy. The film was shot on a minimal budget using a borrowed camera and was produced without institutional support. Riera i Sola interviewed women who worked as street vendors, domestic workers, laundresses, and informal-sector traders, and the film presented their accounts of their economic lives in their own words, without narration or editorial commentary.

The film was screened at the [[Sant Cristòfor Cultural Centre]], a community arts space in the harbour-front district that had established a regular screening programme in 1985, and at community centres and feminist meetings. Its audience was small — the underground's distribution infrastructure was limited — but the film was recognised within the underground film community as a significant departure from the mainstream's treatment of women's lives. The experience of making the documentary, during which Riera i Sola had interviewed women about their economic and sexual lives and had found that the women's accounts of their sexuality were inseparable from their accounts of their economic precarity, provided the conceptual foundation for her subsequent fiction film, ''El Desig''.

=== ''El Desig'' (1987) and the obscenity trial ===

{{main|El Desig (film)|Balboa v. Riera i Sola}}

Riera i Sola wrote the screenplay for ''[[El Desig (film)|El Desig]]'' ("The Desire") over the course of 1985 and 1986, during the peak of the [[Balboa real-estate bubble|real-estate bubble]]. The film depicted the sexual and economic lives of three women in Sant Cristòfor — a cleaner, a secretary, and a bar owner — whose lives intersect through the harbour district and the new financial district. The film's explicit depiction of female desire, same-sex attraction, and abortion constituted the first frank portrayal of female sexuality in a Balboan film.

The film was financed through Riera i Sola's personal savings and small contributions from feminist organisations and individual supporters. It was shot on 16mm film over six weeks in early 1987, using natural light and real locations in the harbour district and the financial district. The cast was drawn from non-professional and community theatre networks: [[Núria Puig i Bosch]], a harbour district resident; [[Aina Fuster i Colom]], a Portblanc-born waitress whose residency in [[Portblanc]] later provided the jurisdictional basis for the re-filing of the obscenity charges; and [[Montserrat Vila i Aragó]], a community theatre performer. [[Bàrbara Serra i Pons]] served as cinematographer. Riera i Sola edited the film herself on a borrowed editing suite.

The film was screened at the Sant Cristòfor Cultural Centre in late 1987 before an audience of approximately 200 people. A report of the screening was forwarded to the Ministry of Justice, and in January 1988 the government initiated prosecution under the [[Public Decency Act]] of 1976 — the first prosecution brought under the Act since its enactment. The case proceeded through four courts over the course of 1988: the Sant Cristòfor High Court dismissed the charges; the case was re-filed at the Portblanc High Court in [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]], which ruled the film obscene; the defence appealed to the [[Supreme Court of Balboa]], which deferred the constitutional question; and the [[Constitutional Court of Balboa]] struck down the Public Decency Act as incompatible with the [[Twelve Guarantees]] and acquitted Riera i Sola on 22 September 1988.

The trial transformed Riera i Sola from an obscure independent filmmaker into a national figure. The case attracted intensive media coverage, and the defence — led by [[Magda Vives i Cardó]] of the [[Balboan Civil Liberties Union]] — drew the mainstream film industry into a public confrontation with the government over the scope of artistic freedom. [[Jordi Molins i Castell]], the mainstream satirical filmmaker, and [[Kwame Rovira]], the Black Balboan subcultural filmmaker, testified for the defence. The defence committee that was organised to support Riera i Sola during the trial reconstituted itself after the acquittal as the [[Cultural Defence Coalition]], a permanent advocacy organisation dedicated to defending artistic freedom in Balboa.

=== ''La Llibertat de les Dones'' (1991) ===

Riera i Sola's third film, ''[[La Llibertat de les Dones (film)|La Llibertat de les Dones]]'' ("The Freedom of Women", 1991), was a more overtly political work that depicted the women's rights movement in Sant Cristòfor and the interior. The film was produced in the aftermath of the trial and was regarded as the most accomplished feminist film of the era. The film traced the interconnected stories of women organisers across the social spectrum — a union activist in the Jala textile mills, a feminist lawyer in Sant Cristòfor, and a rural woman in Tramuntana who challenges her community's patriarchal conventions — and addressed the structural inequalities that persisted despite the formal equality established by the Twelve Guarantees.

The film was screened at the Sant Cristòfor Cultural Centre and circulated through the underground screening circuit. It was regarded by critics as a more mature work than ''El Desig'', displaying greater narrative confidence and a broader social vision, while retaining the directness and the commitment to women's perspectives that characterised Riera i Sola's earlier work.

=== ''El Silenci'' (1996) ===

The [[1994 Balboa spot recession|1994 spot recession]] closed the Sant Cristòfor Cultural Centre and dissolved the underground production companies, including [[Cinema Independent]], that had sustained the underground film community. Riera i Sola, like other underground filmmakers, lost the infrastructure on which her work had depended. Her response was ''El Silenci'' ("The Silence", 1996), a documentary that revisited the women of the harbour district whom she had first interviewed for ''Les Dones del Port'' fifteen years earlier. The film examined the effects of the real-estate crash, the recession, and the dissolution of the underground community on the women's lives, and it was marked by a sombre, reflective tone that contrasted with the urgency of her earlier work.

The film was produced on a minimal budget and was screened at university campuses and community centres, as the underground's distribution infrastructure had largely collapsed. It was the last film Riera i Sola made for over a decade, as the recession and the loss of the underground's infrastructure forced her to scale back her filmmaking activities.

== Later career ==

After ''El Silenci'', Riera i Sola shifted her focus from filmmaking to teaching and advocacy. She conducted film workshops at community centres and universities, and she became involved in the [[Cultural Defence Coalition]], the advocacy organisation that had emerged from the defence committee of the 1988 trial. She also wrote essays on cinema and feminism for ''[[La Veu de la Badia]]'' and other publications, and she participated in the campaigns for the [[Constitutional Amendment on Sexual Orientation Protections]] and the first [[Pride parade|Pride march]] in Sant Cristòfor in 1993.

The gradual recovery of the Balboan film industry in the 2000s, driven by the growth of television production and the emergence of a new generation of independent filmmakers, brought renewed attention to Riera i Sola's work. In 2002, the [[Unió d'Artesans del Cinema]] (UAC) honoured her with a retrospective of her complete filmography at its annual awards ceremony — the first time the UAC had recognised an underground filmmaker with its retrospective programme. The retrospective included a public screening of ''El Desig'' at a mainstream cinema venue, the first time the film had been exhibited in a commercial theatre. In 2003, Riera i Sola received the UAC Lifetime Achievement Award, the union's most prestigious honour.

Riera i Sola returned to filmmaking in 2006 with ''Les Animes'' ("The Souls"), a fiction film about three older women in the harbour district who reflect on their lives and their community in the aftermath of the transformations that had reshaped Sant Cristòfor since the 1970s. The film was produced with the support of a small grant from the Balboan Arts Council and was screened at the UAC Awards ceremony and at independent cinemas in Sant Cristòfor. It was regarded as a contemplative, autumnal work that brought the themes of Riera i Sola's earlier films — economic precarity, sexual autonomy, community displacement — into the register of aging and memory.

In 2007, Riera i Sola released ''La Lesbiana Impopular'' ("The Unpopular Lesbian"), a fictional autobiographical film that transposed her own coming-of-age as a lesbian teenager in [[Sant Bart]] in the 1960s into a 1990s version of the same city. The protagonist, mentally situated in the 1960s, navigates a more accepting and cosmopolitan Sant Bart of the late 1990s, where her sexuality is met with indifference or support, but she lives in fear of persecution shaped by the conservative upbringing that no longer corresponds to the world around her. The film's deliberate anachronisms — the [[No Return movement]] protests of the 1950s and 1960s, for example, appear in the film as "No Return to Black and White TV" — underscore the disjunction between the protagonist's internal world and her external reality. The film received moderate commercial success, grossing $1,104,000 on a budget of $102,400, and was nominated for Best Picture at the [[Unió d'Artesans del Cinema|UAC Awards]] — the first time Riera i Sola's work had been recognised in the UAC's top category. Critics regarded the film as a return to form for Riera i Sola, recapturing the subversive energy of her 1980s underground work.

In 2010, Riera i Sola released ''[[El Desig 2 (film)|El Desig 2]]'', a dark comedy that engaged with the ''El Desig'' trial not through a courtroom dramatisation but through the story of a fictionalised private investigator, hired by a group called the "Not Catholic Church" — a satirical name widely understood to be a front for the [[Balboan Catholic Church]] — to find a jurisdictional connection that would allow the case to be re-filed in [[Portblanc]] after its dismissal. The film's break-in sequence was inspired by a real-life burglary at Riera i Sola's apartment in the late 1980s, which she reimagined as the act of the obsessive investigator. The film was shot digitally on a modest budget of approximately $72,000 and received a limited theatrical release in Sant Cristòfor. Critics regarded it as Riera i Sola's most accessible and most subversive work; the film won the Best Film award at the [[Costa Nord Film Festival]] in 2010 and the top award at the [[Castejón Film Festival]] in 2011, making it the first Balboan film to win the top award at both festivals. Despite the critical acclaim, the film's strangeness limited its commercial appeal — it grossed approximately $247,000 — and it achieved only a brief theatrical run. In the years following its release, the film developed a cult following through home video and streaming platforms.

== Personal life ==

Riera i Sola has lived in the harbour-front district of Sant Cristòfor since the early 1970s. She has been in a long-term relationship with Anna Mas i Puig, a social worker, since 1979. The couple met at a feminist meeting in the harbour district and have lived together in the same neighbourhood throughout Riera i Sola's career. Riera i Sola has been private about her personal life and has declined to discuss her relationship in interviews, though she has acknowledged that her personal experience has informed her films' treatment of women's sexuality and economic precarity.

Riera i Sola's decision to leave Sant Bart for Sant Cristòfor in 1970, and her subsequent decision to leave Cinema Lliure for independent filmmaking, have been interpreted by cultural commentators as expressions of the same impulse that drives her films: a refusal to accept the constraints imposed by institutional authority — whether the patriarchal conventions of the interior or the commercial imperatives of the mainstream studio system — on women's autonomy and self-expression.

== Legacy ==

Montserrat Riera i Sola is widely regarded as the leading feminist filmmaker of the [[Gilded age of Balboan cinema]] and one of the most significant figures in the history of [[Cinema of Balboa|Balboan cinema]]. Her films — particularly ''El Desig'' and ''La Llibertat de les Dones'' — are studied for their artistic significance, for their role in the social movements of the period, and for their documentation of the experiences of women in the post-reunification Balboan state. The [[Balboa v. Riera i Sola|obscenity trial]] that resulted from ''El Desig'' is regarded as a landmark in the development of Balboan civil liberties, and the Constitutional Court's ruling in the case established the precedent that artistic expression is protected by the [[Twelve Guarantees]].

Riera i Sola's influence on subsequent Balboan filmmakers is widely acknowledged. The triptych structure, the use of non-professional actors, and the integration of economic and sexual themes that characterised ''El Desig'' established a formal and thematic template that subsequent underground and independent filmmakers adopted and adapted. The production of ''[[Els Amagats (film)|Els Amagats]]'' (1989), the first significant LGBT film of the era, was directly enabled by the acquittal in the ''El Desig'' trial, and the broader output of [[Cinema Independent]] in the late 1980s and early 1990s drew on the artistic and political precedents that Riera i Sola's work established.

The UAC's belated recognition of Riera i Sola — the retrospective in 2002 and the Lifetime Achievement Award in 2003 — has been regarded as a corrective to the mainstream industry's exclusion of the underground during the Gilded age. The UAC has subsequently expanded its membership criteria to include filmmakers whose work was distributed outside the mainstream theatre system, a reform that has been attributed in part to the reassessment prompted by Riera i Sola's retrospective.

